Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Sentinel Foundry**, a wide, open-fronted casting works
whose gantry crucible tips and pours molten metal into a casting bed while great
bellows breathe beside it — as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only the
`voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting discrete opaque cells** — one recorded
operation at a time — and you **author its pour and bellows animations** as F-curves. It
melts and pours metal; it does not hammer or stamp.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Foundry is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how
you break the hall into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape
every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a pouring, breathing
foundry needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json`
— together with the rig you define are your submission.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels are
**opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Each call **records only** —
it appends the operation to that part's log and renders nothing; run `voxel-anim
render` when you want to (re)draw the per-part previews (`parts/<part>.png`) and the
assembled scene.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `voxel-anim define-part`
(giving each a parent and a pivot), place the joints they move on with
`voxel-anim define-joint`, and author the required animations with `define-animation`/
`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so
define a part before you paint into it; each part is a **separate** volume in the
shared coordinates. The field starts **empty**.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name hall
voxel-anim fill-box --part hall --x0 1 --y0 0 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 5 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part names above are just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig and animation subcommands
(defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Run
`voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to see that part's progress **and
the assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole foundry composed from all your parts,
drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the crucible hangs over the casting bed and the bellows
seat on the flank. Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates,
position each part where it belongs on the finished hall; the scene simply composes
them there.

