## What to deliver

Build and rig the foundry up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a wide, low, open-fronted masonry casting hall, a
gantry-slung crucible brimming with molten metal over a casting bed, and great bellows
on the flank. **Author the two required animations** as F-curves with the `voxel-anim`
animation subcommands: `crucible_pour` (a full crucible that tips forward on its
trunnions, pours a stream of molten metal into the casting bed, holds, and eases back
level — with weight, not a weightless linear slide) and `bellows_breathe` (a slow,
weighted squeeze-shut and draw-open that loops seamlessly). Both are self-playing and
looping, so the foundry runs on its own with no caller, and nothing hammers or stamps.
The animations are the fixed contract — produce both, by those names; the parts and
joints that realize them are yours. Before you finish, run `voxel-anim render` so it
emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part is
scored as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one part; `voxel-anim
render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant of an
animation to check the motion). When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs
and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
